{"reference":"Acts 28","book":"Acts","chapter":28,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Acts ends in Rome with Paul teaching openly from rented lodging despite being under guard. The narrative does not report the outcome of his trial or his death, so its ending leaves those later questions unanswered.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:16-31"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_8b9a7e47ae8b21ca3eecbf21"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"Rome is more than the final setting: Acts has earlier traced a geographic movement of witness from Jerusalem through Judea and Samaria and outward. The closing picture of proclamation in Rome gives that movement its final narrative setting without requiring the story to narrate Paul’s later fate.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:30-31"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_4652cea1b1639089fc9f0d5c"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions Raised by the Ending","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The abrupt ending has prompted debate. Acts is commonly discussed as ancient historiographic narrative, though it also invites comparison with biography, travel writing, institutional origins, and apologetic writing. Proposals for when it was written differ, and Paul’s fate after this chapter must be reconstructed, if at all, from later letters and reception traditions rather than from Acts itself.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:30-31"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_7e8ae0edf7b09b5db49949d6"]},{"id":"block_4","text":"Paul’s guarded lodging is the last stage of a longer Acts narrative involving arrest, military intervention, hearings, an appeal to Caesar, travel, and custody. This background helps explain why he can receive visitors and speak freely while still being constrained; it should not be used to treat every narrated procedure as an exact legal precedent or to portray imperial justice as uniformly benevolent.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:16-31"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_20bdbda7b5720a6a583a20eb"]}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Rome, Communities, and Meeting Places","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The places named on the approach to Rome—Puteoli, the Forum of Appius, and the Three Taverns—fit a port-and-road corridor and help make sense of fellow believers meeting the travelers before they reach the city. Roman Jewish life also included communities, synagogues, Sabbath study, and ties to Jerusalem, giving concrete context for Paul’s meeting with Jewish leaders in Acts 28. These details do not make all Roman Jews a single, uniform group.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:11-16","Acts 28:17-28","Acts 28:30-31"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_4a0f6145e89e28155b885c05"]},{"id":"block_6","text":"Paul’s residence should be pictured as guarded rented lodging, not automatically as a subterranean prison. No archaeological site securely identifies this lodging, and later Roman churches, prisons, tombs, and other memorials preserve later reception rather than independently locating the events of Acts 28. Roman urban housing ranged from elite homes to multi-storey apartment buildings, shops, workshops, warehouses, and other rented spaces; this broadens the possible setting for the visitors whom Paul receives.","verse_refs":["Acts 28:11-16","Acts 28:16-31","Acts 28:30-31"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_5a5a82b4e793d4705649c78c","reader_path_acts_028_a33ff9f2e7de0a7bc9139232"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The “We” Passages","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"The first-person plural language in this chapter belongs to a wider pattern in Acts 16, 20–21, and 27–28. Its source and what it implies about authorship remain disputed. Textual witnesses to Acts also include a substantially distinctive form often called the Western text, whose individual readings require case-by-case judgment.","verse_refs":["Acts 10:1-48","Acts 11:1-30","Acts 12:1-25","Acts 13:1-52","Acts 14:1-28","Acts 15:1-41","Acts 16:1-40","Acts 17:1-34","Acts 18:1-28","Acts 19:1-41","Acts 1:1-26","Acts 20:1-38","Acts 21:1-40","Acts 22:1-30","Acts 23:1-35","Acts 24:1-27","Acts 25:1-27","Acts 26:1-32","Acts 27:1-44","Acts 2:1-47","Acts 3:1-26","Acts 4:1-37","Acts 5:1-42","Acts 6:1-15","Acts 7:1-60","Acts 8:1-40","Acts 9:1-43"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_028_d95d2263c445854db045b2eb"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}